after the second day of Kampala scape  the violence are starting to return to  their battered neighborhoods in  Northeast Delhi in Schenck bag they've  come back to widespread destruction and  loss it was very stressful living here  during the last few days we were all  extremely scared so scared that we moved  all our woman to a safer place only the  men stayed people were killed and  injured here homes and businesses were  burned Mohammed's fruit and vegetable  shop provided a livelihood for his  family for 30 years he was burned during  the riots and he's not insured he is  part of the Muslim community that  suffered the worst of the violence but  he's not vengeful we have to live  peacefully together whatever has  happened we have to treat it as a  one-off we have to forget we can't just  blame the Hindus or Muslims both groups  were there three shops down is his  neighbor Ganesh it was a Hindu his fruit  and veg shop was also burnt but even  after losing his livelihood he still  believes in coexistence when there is  peace we can once again start our  business we have to do it to survive to  earn our living there is no other way to  earn a living it's a seed of hope that  Hindus and Muslims can return to the  peaceful coexistence they enjoyed for  years a senior police commissioner  visited Chang bag Thursday to reassure  locals that peace has been restored but  it's the presence of large numbers of  security forces that people find more  reassuring not words are known this is a  very public confidence-building measure  designed to show people that the  security forces have control of the  streets and it's now safe but many  people are asking the question where  were they when they needed them most  Delhi Police have been heavily  criticised over its handling of the  violence which has been described as a  best negligent and worse complicit  families are slowly returning to this  battleground feeling more apprehensive  than safe it seems to be peace and calm  but we lost everything after our home  was set on fire we only managed to  salvage this cooking gas  but others like this Hindu family have  decided to leave the district altogether  the riot situation has scared us a lot  that's why we're going back to our  village where it's safer the streets  will be cleared the debris removed and  homes rebuilt but these communities  cannot fully heal until those  responsible face justice  Tony Berkeley al Jazeera Shan bag  northeast Delhi
 

